      ":question/id": "7759fe8d-16ba-5c19-a729-5ce624aeef6f",
      ":question/problem": "If $\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=-6$ and $\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)=k$, then $\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y\\right)=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y\\right) &=\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right) \\\\ &=2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=-6$ and $\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)=k$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=2k+(-6)=2k-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y\\right)=2k-6.\n$$",
